 my my son's what  they were played with and they were  playing with action  figures and I started thinking well it  should be an action figure  because that's really what what is is  what kids are playing with and then I I  just remembered back to two seminal  things in my life one I grew up during  the 60s when when the space race was  going on and and the astronauts were  going to the moon and they were the  coolest guys on the planet when they  walked out my parents would get me up  and we'd watch them live on TV walking  out to get in that elevator to go up and  climb into that capsule to go to the  moon and these guys were walking in the  white suits with the clear helmets and  they had the kind of little skull caps  on with it with you know the the ear you  know the the microphones and the  headphones on and so and you sit there  and they were so cool and I thought oh  my god that's great so I never forgot  that and also Star Wars which was such  an important film for all of us really  but it but it was so so so important you  know in in kind of space becoming sir  vibrant storytelling again and and so so  I thought well it should be a space  superhero a you know kind of space  action figure kind of superhero guy and  that's where the evolution from from the  tin toy went and then it was bud lucky  who was one of the great story guys that  that worked with us who was older than  us who's from Montana and he said well  have you thought about the ventriloquist  dummy being a cowboy and right away I  knew that was perfect because in Buddy  pictures you have to make the the the  the two characters in the beginning of  the film as opposite as possible you  know the one that was really important  to us was the Defiant ones starring  Sidney Poitier and Tony Curtis you know  and and it's in a prison in the south  where you know a black man from the  north is handcuffed to to erases from  from the south and they get into it  they're being transferred they get it to  an exit and they escape the white guy  wants to go south the black guy wants to  go north you can't get more opposite  than that and it's the most powerful  film it's not a comedy it's a drama but  you just to see these two grow together  and they're literally handcuffed to  gather and it's so powerful and that's  what's so inspiring for us with this and  so that really became the foundation of  the story that we started to tell so we  get we start developing the story and  everyone at Disney was really concerned  that that that adults teenagers college  students they would why would they come  to a movie to see toys being played with  by a kid and having the word toy in the  title it's like no one's gonna see this  is too juvenile you got to make it edgy  or you got to make it edgy or you got to  make it you know they kept saying edgy  and you got to make it edgy you know and  so we're okay you know clearly they know  you know we're never done a feature and  we're drawing away and trying to make it  and so he kept pushing the you know  woody woody the main character the the  character Tom Hanks was going to play to  be to be edgy and edgy and edgy and then  we we finally put together like the  first act of the film and we showed it  to Disney with all this you know notes  that they were given that we had to do  mandatory notes and and so this is an  image kind of during that time and woody  was not likable at all and we were we we  we thought okay this is we gate we did  their notes and we showed it to him and  it was a disaster  it was horrible we call it Black Friday  and and it was it was really horrible  they they said what happened this is  like kind of unlikable unwatchable oh my  god and and and they were they were  threatening they wanted to shut the  production down I also got a call from  Peter Schneider maybe sitting in my car  telling him that we had to move Pixar  down to Burbank to finish the film they  thought you clearly can't do it yourself  we're g